# vox - allow the dictation service to create a virtual keyboard.
#
# vox types transcripts through a uinput virtual keyboard. That is the only
# approach that works identically on X11 and on every Wayland compositor:
# xdotool is X11-only, and wtype needs a protocol GNOME and KDE do not
# implement. The tradeoff is that it needs write access to /dev/uinput.
#
# SECURITY, PLAINLY: write access to /dev/uinput is the ability to synthesize
# arbitrary keystrokes into the active session. That is inherent to what a
# dictation tool does -- it exists to type for you -- but it means any process
# with this access can type anything, including into a sudo prompt.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" scopes it to the currently active LOCAL session and revokes on
# logout, which is narrower than GROUP="input" and much narrower than adding
# yourself to a group permanently. Note uaccess is implemented by
# systemd-logind; on OpenRC, runit or s6 it does nothing and you will need the
# group-based line instead.
#
# If you would rather not grant this at all, vox falls back to wtype, ydotool
# or xdotool. `vox doctor` reports which it would use.

ACTION=="remove", GOTO="vox_end"
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
LABEL="vox_end"
